1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Every vaccine in the world is named after
a cow.

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:08,380
So how did a cow end up inside the most
important medicine ever made?

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:14,967
By the end of this film, you'll know how a
milkmaid's hand, a country doctor and one

4
00:00:15,007 --> 00:00:20,681
careful test turned an old farmyard rumour
into the medicine that wiped a disease off

5
00:00:20,721 --> 00:00:22,200
the face of the Earth.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,340
In the seventeen-hundreds, the most feared
disease in the world was smallpox.

7
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:31,200
People called it the speckled monster.

8
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:36,909
It caught kings and farm workers alike,
and about three in every ten people who

9
00:00:36,949 --> 00:00:37,900
caught it died.

10
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,960
Many who lived were scarred for life, and
some were left blind.

11
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,920
Picture yourself in an English village in
those years.

12
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:52,911
When smallpox arrived, you would see a
neighbour's house shut up and silent, hear

13
00:00:52,951 --> 00:00:57,720
the church bell toll day after day, and
wonder whose house would be next.

14
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:02,840
Parents counted their children only after
they had survived it.

15
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,480
There was one way to fight back, and it
was risky.

16
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,993
Doctors took a little matter from a
smallpox sore and scratched it into a

17
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:13,280
healthy person's arm.

18
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:17,700
It usually gave a milder illness, and
lifelong protection.

19
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,020
It was called variolation.

20
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:25,220
But some people died from it, and they
could pass full smallpox on to others.

21
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,300
So people faced a terrible choice.

22
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:32,060
Risk the treatment, or wait for the
monster to come.

23
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:35,360
If it were your child, which would you
choose?

24
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:40,000
In the countryside, though, farmers had
noticed something strange.

25
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:46,250
Cows sometimes caught a disease of their
own, called cowpox.

26
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:49,290
It made small blisters on their udders.

27
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:54,010
And the milkmaids who milked them
sometimes caught it too, on their hands.

28
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:56,210
It was a mild illness.

29
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:01,130
A few sore spots, a day or two of feeling
unwell, and that was all.

30
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:05,510
But in the dairy country, people said
something odd.

31
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:10,950
Anyone who had had cowpox would never
catch smallpox.

32
00:02:11,970 --> 00:02:15,050
Imagine being a milkmaid in a smallpox
year.

33
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:20,305
While your neighbours lock their doors,
you walk to market without fear, because

34
00:02:20,345 --> 00:02:23,270
you had a few sore spots on your hands
from the cows.

35
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:26,310
It sounds like an old wives' tale.

36
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:28,370
But it kept coming true.

37
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:31,790
Some farmers even acted on it.

38
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:38,473
In seventeen seventy-four, a Dorset farmer
called Benjamin Jesty scratched cowpox

39
00:02:38,513 --> 00:02:42,930
into the arms of his wife and his two
sons, to protect them.

40
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:48,971
It worked, but he told almost no one, and
he never wrote it down for doctors to

41
00:02:49,011 --> 00:02:49,811
read.

42
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:55,818
Not far away, in the little town of
Berkeley, a country doctor called Edward

43
00:02:55,858 --> 00:02:59,310
Jenner had heard the same story from his
patients.

44
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:02,070
Jenner was a curious man.

45
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:07,839
He studied cuckoos and hedgehogs, sent up
one of the first hydrogen balloons ever

46
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:10,970
seen in England, and wrote poems for his
friends.

47
00:03:12,090 --> 00:03:15,830
But the milkmaids' story was the puzzle he
could not leave alone.

48
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:20,730
As a boy, Jenner had been put through
variolation himself.

49
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:23,530
He knew how hard it was.

50
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:29,700
His old teacher in London, the surgeon
John Hunter, had once given him some

51
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:30,690
famous advice.

52
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:32,670
Why think?

53
00:03:33,290 --> 00:03:34,850
Why not try the experiment?

54
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:41,655
So Jenner started collecting cases, of
people who had caught cowpox and then

55
00:03:41,695 --> 00:03:43,410
never caught smallpox.

56
00:03:44,430 --> 00:03:46,210
For years, he wrote them down.

57
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:49,070
But a list of stories isn't proof.

58
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:51,430
Jenner needed a test.

59
00:03:54,270 --> 00:04:00,404
In May seventeen ninety-six, a young
dairymaid called Sarah Nelmes came to

60
00:04:00,444 --> 00:04:03,030
Jenner with cowpox on her hand.

61
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:06,590
She had caught it from a cow on a nearby
farm.

62
00:04:07,230 --> 00:04:10,330
A cow later remembered by the name
Blossom.

63
00:04:11,470 --> 00:04:13,170
Jenner saw his chance.

64
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:19,695
On the fourteenth of May, he took a little
matter from one of the spots on Sarah's

65
00:04:19,735 --> 00:04:25,470
hand, and placed it in two small scratches
on the arm of a boy called James Phipps.

66
00:04:26,230 --> 00:04:32,022
In his own notes, Jenner described him
simply as a healthy boy, about eight years

67
00:04:32,062 --> 00:04:32,862
old.

68
00:04:33,610 --> 00:04:36,390
Over the next week, James felt a little
unwell.

69
00:04:36,930 --> 00:04:41,050
A slight fever, a poor appetite, a night
of restlessness.

70
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:46,130
Then, just as Jenner had hoped, he was
perfectly well again.

71
00:04:47,410 --> 00:04:51,130
Picture Jenner in those days, watching the
boy run about the garden.

72
00:04:51,970 --> 00:04:56,450
If the old saying was wrong, he had given
a child a disease for nothing.

73
00:04:57,190 --> 00:05:02,650
If it was right, he might be holding the
key to the most feared illness on Earth.

74
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:06,110
So James had caught cowpox.

75
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:09,130
Now came the question that really
mattered.

76
00:05:09,810 --> 00:05:12,050
Would it protect him from smallpox?

77
00:05:14,930 --> 00:05:17,210
On the first of July, Jenner did the test.

78
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:22,819
He gave James a standard variolation, the
old treatment that used real smallpox

79
00:05:22,859 --> 00:05:26,630
matter, which would normally cause at
least a mild illness.

80
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:28,330
Then he waited.

81
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:29,870
One day.

82
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:30,990
Three days.

83
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:32,430
A week.

84
00:05:33,290 --> 00:05:34,090
Nothing happened.

85
00:05:34,890 --> 00:05:37,450
In Jenner's words, no disease followed.

86
00:05:38,490 --> 00:05:39,930
Months later, he tried again.

87
00:05:40,350 --> 00:05:41,150
Still nothing.

88
00:05:41,710 --> 00:05:43,690
The cowpox had protected the boy.

89
00:05:43,730 --> 00:05:46,490
Jenner wasn't the first to try cowpox.

90
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:49,910
Benjamin Jesty had done it twenty-two
years earlier.

91
00:05:50,490 --> 00:05:55,723
What Jenner did that was new was test it,
carefully, and then publish every case, so

92
00:05:55,763 --> 00:05:59,090
any doctor could check his work and do it
themselves.

93
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:04,472
He tested more people, and in seventeen
ninety-eight he paid to print a short

94
00:06:04,512 --> 00:06:04,810
book.

95
00:06:05,270 --> 00:06:11,965
He called the disease by a Latin name,
variolae vaccinae, which means smallpox of

96
00:06:12,005 --> 00:06:12,805
the cow.

97
00:06:13,310 --> 00:06:18,670
From vacca, the Latin word for cow, came
the name for the treatment: vaccination.

98
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:21,270
Not everyone believed him.

99
00:06:21,690 --> 00:06:26,293
Some doctors mocked the idea, and
cartoonists drew people sprouting little

100
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:28,090
cow's heads after their jab.

101
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:30,850
But the results were hard to argue with.

102
00:06:31,610 --> 00:06:36,665
Jenner sent cowpox matter to anyone who
asked, and in a little hut in his own

103
00:06:36,705 --> 00:06:40,410
garden in Berkeley he vaccinated poor
families for free.

104
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:45,589
Within a few years, doctors were
vaccinating people across Britain, Europe

105
00:06:45,629 --> 00:06:46,630
and the Americas.

106
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:54,066
A lifetime later, the French scientist
Louis Pasteur made new treatments that

107
00:06:54,106 --> 00:06:56,510
trained the body to fight other diseases.

108
00:06:57,490 --> 00:07:02,190
In honour of Jenner's cowpox, he proposed
calling them all by the same name.

109
00:07:02,870 --> 00:07:03,670
Vaccines.

110
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:09,290
So every vaccine, for every disease, still
carries the name of a cow.

111
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:12,650
And Jenner's idea went further than he
could have dreamed.

112
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:18,120
In the twentieth century, health workers
travelled to every country where smallpox

113
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:22,754
still lived, vaccinating everyone around
each new case, until there was nowhere

114
00:07:22,794 --> 00:07:23,850
left for it to go.

115
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:29,148
On the eighth of May, nineteen eighty, the
world's health leaders declared that

116
00:07:29,188 --> 00:07:30,190
smallpox was gone.

117
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:35,708
A disease that had killed hundreds of
millions of people over the centuries had

118
00:07:35,748 --> 00:07:38,590
been wiped out, the first ever ended by
humans.

119
00:07:39,890 --> 00:07:41,210
Why was this so groundbreaking?

120
00:07:42,170 --> 00:07:47,376
Because it showed that the body can learn
to fight a disease before it ever meets

121
00:07:47,416 --> 00:07:47,610
it.

122
00:07:48,250 --> 00:07:53,136
A mild, safe cousin of an illness can
teach your body to recognise the real

123
00:07:53,176 --> 00:07:53,976
thing.

124
00:07:54,430 --> 00:07:58,810
That one idea is behind every vaccine you
have ever had.

125
00:07:59,650 --> 00:08:04,014
And because no one alive today needs a
vaccine against smallpox, most of us have

126
00:08:04,054 --> 00:08:05,210
never seen it at all.

127
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:09,950
The speckled monster is something you will
only ever meet in a history book.

128
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:15,070
A milkmaid's hand, a country doctor, and a
boy who stayed well.

129
00:08:16,210 --> 00:08:20,998
Together, they turned a farmyard rumour
into proof, and gave a cow's name to

130
00:08:21,038 --> 00:08:21,838
medicine.

131
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:26,930
Long before Jenner, another mystery
frightened people every summer.

132
00:08:27,730 --> 00:08:32,030
To solve it, a printer in Philadelphia
flew a kite into a thunderstorm.
